**user**:
> On Mac OS, how can I set up a program to be run every day at a particular time, and pipe its results into a log file (ideally, named for that days date in a folder of them)?
**assistant**:
> You can accomplish this by using the `launchd` daemon, which is the recommended way to schedule tasks on macOS. Here’s a step-by-step guide to set up a daily task:
> 
> 1. **Create a Launch Daemon Property List (plist) File**: You will need to create a `.plist` file that describes the scheduled task. Here's an example `com.yourusername.dailyprogram.plist` file that runs a script every day at 7:00 AM:
> 
>     ```xml
>     <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
>     <!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
>     <plist version="1.0">
>     <dict>
>         <key>Label</key>
>         <string>com.yourusername.dailyprogram</string>
> 
>         <key>ProgramArguments</key>
>         <array>
>             <string>/path/to/your/script.sh</string>
>         </array>
> 
>         <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
>         <dict>
>             <key>Hour</key>
>             <integer>7</integer>
>             <key>Minute</key>
>             <integer>0</integer>
>         </dict>
> 
>         <key>StandardOutPath</key>
>         <string>/path/to/log/folder/$(date '+%Y-%m-%d').log</string>
>         
>         <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
>         <string>/path/to/log/folder/$(date '+%Y-%m-%d').log</string>
>     </dict>
>     </plist>
>     ```
> 
>     - Replace `/path/to/your/script.sh` with the path to the script or program you want to run.
>     - Replace `/path/to/log/folder/` with the path to the folder where you want the log files to be saved.
> 
> 2. **Place the PLIST File in the Proper Directory**: 
> 
>     - For user-specific tasks, place this file in `~/Library/LaunchAgents/`.
>     - For system-wide tasks, use `/Library/LaunchDaemons/`. You’ll need administrative privileges for this.
> 
> 3. **Ensure the Script Is Executable**: If your program is a script, make sure it is executable:
> 
>     ```bash
>     chmod +x /path/to/your/script.sh
>     ```
> 
> 4. **Load the PLIST File with launchctl**: After placing the plist file in the correct directory, load it with:
> 
>     ```bash
>     launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.yourusername.dailyprogram.plist
>     ```
> 
> 5. **Verify and Troubleshoot**: Check if your job is loaded and working properly with:
> 
>     ```bash
>     launchctl list | grep com.yourusername.dailyprogram
>     ```
> 
> If you need to edit the plist file and reload it, remember to unload it first:
> 
> ```bash
> launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.yourusername.dailyprogram.plist
> ```
> 
> Note that in the plist file, `$(date '+%Y-%m-%d')` is a shell command and won't work directly within the `plist` file. You would usually handle date-based naming within the script itself:
> 
> ```bash
> #!/bin/bash
> log_directory="/path/to/log/folder"
> log_filename="$(date '+%Y-%m-%d').log"
> # Your program/command here
> echo "Running your program..." > "$log_directory/$log_filename"
> ```
> 
> You would replace the `echo "Running your program..."` line with the actual command you want to execute. This approach ensures the log file is named according to the date on each run.
